      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-146454",
      ":question/problem": "It is known that $15%$ of a particular teenage population ride bicycles for exercise. A random sample of $n = 60$ teenagers is taken from the population and asked whether they ride a bicycle for exercise.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $p^(^)$, the proportion of teenagers in the sample who ride bicycles? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
